WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.360
How can a small mark change what a protein does without rebuilding it?

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:07.680
Watch this population of protein copies.

3
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.900
Each copy flickers among shapes, and those shapes make different contacts.

4
00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:17.540
The cell will change the odds, not manufacture a replacement.

5
00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.340
That is faster than waiting to make new protein.

6
00:00:24.833 --> 00:00:29.633
A kinase brings ATP beside a hydroxyl-bearing site on a protein.

7
00:00:30.773 --> 00:00:34.013
The kinase transfers the end phosphate, leaving ADP.

8
00:00:35.493 --> 00:00:40.373
In animal signaling, the best-studied targets are serine, threonine, and tyrosine.

9
00:00:41.573 --> 00:00:45.813
The protein remains the same chain, but its local chemistry has changed.

10
00:00:47.073 --> 00:00:50.373
This attachment is a reversible covalent modification.

11
00:00:51.667 --> 00:00:56.547
Near neutral pH, the attached phosphate carries a strong negative charge.

12
00:00:57.847 --> 00:01:00.307
Put that charge among the protein's other charges.

13
00:01:01.787 --> 00:01:05.107
Attractions strengthen in some arrangements and weaken in others.

14
00:01:06.367 --> 00:01:10.947
The population shifts toward shapes and contacts that accommodate the new charge.

15
00:01:12.607 --> 00:01:14.407
Partners can also read the phosphate's shape.

16
00:01:15.767 --> 00:01:20.027
Phosphate changes probabilities; it does not issue a universal command.

17
00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:22.410
Here is your prediction.

18
00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:28.890
If the chemical mark is always the same, must phosphorylation always activate its

19
00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:29.890
target protein?

20
00:01:35.817 --> 00:01:39.557
Phosphorylation shifts glycogen phosphorylase toward activity.

21
00:01:40.857 --> 00:01:43.837
It inhibits glycogen synthase, which builds glycogen.

22
00:01:45.057 --> 00:01:47.737
The direction depends on the protein and the site.

23
00:01:49.057 --> 00:01:53.237
The same charge can favor a different shape or disrupt a different contact.

24
00:01:54.367 --> 00:01:59.027
Sometimes the important change is a contact, not catalytic activity.

25
00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:03.987
A partner can recognize the phosphorylated surface and dock.

26
00:02:05.327 --> 00:02:08.727
Elsewhere, the mark can expose or hide a location signal.

27
00:02:09.987 --> 00:02:13.567
A phosphate can also prepare a nearby site for another kinase.

28
00:02:14.827 --> 00:02:17.867
These are different outcomes of the same physical change.

29
00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.860
The mark lasts until an enzyme removes it.

30
00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:28.340
A phosphatase uses water to release inorganic phosphate and restore the unmodified

31
00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:33.440
site. It does not reverse the kinase reaction or remake ATP.

32
00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:39.580
Writing and erasing are separate reactions, controlled by separate enzymes.

33
00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:43.480
A complete trip spends an ATP molecule.

34
00:02:44.583 --> 00:02:48.023
That separation makes the mark both stable and reversible.

35
00:02:49.463 --> 00:02:53.543
ATP is plentiful enough that a kinase rarely waits for its donor.

36
00:02:54.823 --> 00:02:59.303
The phosphate persists without an eraser, yet can disappear quickly when a

37
00:02:59.303 --> 00:03:05.983
phosphatase acts. Cells can therefore alter existing proteins faster than rebuilding

38
00:03:05.983 --> 00:03:07.123
the whole population.

39
00:03:08.283 --> 00:03:10.403
Carry this rule into every case.

40
00:03:11.723 --> 00:03:16.243
Ask where the phosphate appears, which shapes or partners it favors, and what

41
00:03:16.243 --> 00:03:22.563
removal restores. The outcome may be activity, inhibition, docking, movement, or

42
00:03:22.563 --> 00:03:28.283
priming. The common cause is a stable phosphate, read through its charge and shape,

43
00:03:28.283 --> 00:03:29.963
that an enzyme can erase.
